{
  "image_name": "0e55eb5c698baf83690424bfc5fd19c8.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how DNA damage can trigger autophagy through several interconnected signaling pathways. On the left, DNA damage activates stress-response proteins such as ATM, ATR, and p53. These signals feed into multiple routes that promote autophagy, including PTEN/AKT regulation, PARP1-driven increases in AMP, and JNK signaling. A central theme is activation of AMPK, which appears to act as an energy sensor linking DNA damage to autophagy initiation.\n\nAMPK then inhibits mTORC1, while AKT and TSC2 also regulate this same node. mTORC1 is shown as a major brake on autophagy, suppressing the ULK/ATG1 complex. Once this inhibition is relieved, the ATG1 complex and a Beclin 1–VPS34 complex drive formation of pre-autophagosomal structures. LC3 is incorporated into the growing membrane, leading to autophagosome formation.\n\nOn the right, the completed autophagosome engulfs cellular material, including what appears to be organelles such as mitochondria, and then fuses with a lysosome for degradation. Overall, the image highlights autophagy as a downstream cellular response to genotoxic stress, controlled largely through AMPK–mTOR signaling and core ATG machinery.",
